WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:07.160
Speaker 1: Kentucky has now essentially banned
abortion in the state.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.990
This is almost certainly a violation of Roe
v. Wade, but the Supreme Court is going to

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:18.109
rule in what is at this point only weeks,
whether Roe v. Wade is essentially gone.

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:24.000
And that's the really scary part of this Vox
reporting it is now practically impossible

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.160
to get an abortion in Kentucky.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:34.950
A new law is forcing the state's last remaining
abortion providers to shut down Vox, reporting

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:41.149
Kentucky ended virtually all in-state abortions
Wednesday, enacting a sweeping law that bans

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:47.129
abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy restricts
minors access to the procedure and cracks

00:00:47.129 --> 00:00:49.220
down on medication abortions.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:54.980
It's now the state with the harshest abortion
restrictions in the United States.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.260
The new law goes into effect immediately.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:02.309
It will force the state's two remaining abortion
clinics in Louisville to close due to onerous

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:08.780
new requirements on doctors, forcing Kentuckians
to look elsewhere for abortion care.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.070
So again, this almost certainly violates Roe
v Wade.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:22.320
Roe v. Wade says that the Constitution protects
women's liberty to choose to have an abortion

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.370
without excessive government restriction.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:31.770
Now, excessive government restriction is the
source of much controversy and opens itself

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.630
up to significant interpretation.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:39.470
So excessive restriction allows some restriction,
but not too much.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:46.140
And under Roe v. Wade closing, putting such
onerous requirements on abortion providers

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:52.990
such that they all close would without a doubt
in any sensible interpretation of Roe v. Wade,

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.850
be considered to be unconstitutional.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:00.491
That's onerous if the if the requirements
are so strict that every provider in the state

00:02:00.491 --> 00:02:05.280
closes and people seeking an abortion have
to go to a different state.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.060
That would violate Roe v. Wade by any sensible
interpretation.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:13.390
But this will be appealed, of course.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:19.470
But in so in that process, during that process,
the Supreme Court might decide in June we

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:26.510
are going to effectively overturn Roe v. Wade
by allowing extremely harsh state laws to

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:27.510
stand.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:33.450
Roe v. Wade could be gone in a few weeks and
as is often the case in red states that are

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.040
trying to ban abortion.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:40.730
Many of those states are an economic and educational
and health care disaster.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:47.380
I've said this so many times before the states
that are obsessed with these cultural issues

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:49.450
are disastrous states.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:55.560
By almost every metric, if you look at Kentucky,
for example, Kentucky is 43 out of 50 in terms

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.320
of health care.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:01.810
Kentucky is 40th out of 50 in terms of its
economy.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:12.030
Kentucky is 48th out of 50 in fiscal stability,
44th in education and 47th in terms of poverty

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.030
rate.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:17.070
And yet their focus is let's try to ban abortion.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:24.180
We've talked before at minimum, at minimum,
if you want to ban abortion, you should include

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:30.970
all sorts of support for the expectant mothers
and parents because all of these additional

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:38.900
children being born that folks in theory wanted
to not have born.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.140
So you're going to give them financial support,
right?

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:42.180
Well, no, no, no.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.500
That's not right, either.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:44.630
And this all ties back.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.780
I haven't used I used to talk about this a
lot.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.110
I haven't for some period of time.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.260
This is what's called strict father morality.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:57.500
You know, the strict dad who says, Well, it's
the principle, it's the principle.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.090
They want to control women's bodies.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:06.110
They have a religious view on, you know, sexual
intercourse is supposed to be strictly for

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:07.770
procreation.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.530
And they're against comprehensive sex education.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.720
And often they're against contraception.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.370
So so that doesn't always.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:20.230
But you don't want abortion, but you also
don't want to do the things to prevent unintended

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:21.230
pregnancy.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:22.230
What do you want?

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:27.350
And they say, Well, we want procreative sex
in the context of marriage.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:33.070
And anything outside of that means one needs
to suffer the consequences.

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:34.210
That's the right morality.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:38.990
If you get pregnant, you've got to have the
kid and you've got to deal with paying for

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.690
all of the expenses of the kid as well.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:47.960
Wait, but so it doesn't make sense, because
we know that one of the best ways to prevent

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:54.140
unwanted pregnancy is total and complete access
to contraception.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.750
Comprehensive sex education.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:57.930
That's how you prevent unwanted pregnancy.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.450
Excellent study out of St. Louis looked at
this.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:05.280
Oh no, but that wouldn't be the right morality,
because that would open the door to people

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.490
just having sex.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:08.490
And that no, no, no.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:10.680
That doesn't go with our morality.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.830
It's completely backwards.

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:17.030
It is antiquated thinking it is non-science
thinking.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:22.500
But horrifyingly and particularly if the Supreme
Court decision at the end of June effectively

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:28.940
reverses Roe v. Wade, this is going to be
the law of the land in many, many red states.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:34.020
Now, as I've said before, what should the
people in these states do?

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:38.840
Because on the one hand, one approach would
be, well, we need progressives and center

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:45.480
left Democrats and whatever to move to these
states to change the laws by becoming involved

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:46.660
in local government.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.510
Yeah, but that's a big uphill battle.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:54.090
It's almost easier to say to the folks who
are rational and clear thinking leave those

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.530
states and go to a blue state.

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:59.500
But the problem is, a lot of people can't
afford to do that.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.490
So it really is a disastrous situation.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:06.930
I don't know what the abortion landscape is
going to look like, say, 12 months from now.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:10.670
A big piece of that is because we don't know
what the Supreme Court will decide in a few

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:11.670
weeks.

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:16.110
And another part of that is we don't know
to what degree red states will continue pushing

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:17.860
these types of laws.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:22.080
Every indication is that red states are determined
to push this stuff.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:28.230
Another reason yet as if we needed more reasons,
yet another reason why the upcoming midterm

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:31.010
elections are so consequential.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:35.200
And by that, I mean, it's not just about who
we elect to the U.S. Senate and the U.S. House

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.830
of Representatives, but there are tons of
local elections as well that are going to

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:40.070
set the tone.

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:44.570
Governors races, state senates state houses
of representatives.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:45.710
We will talk about it more.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:49.080
We'll talk about what's at stake, but it is
a very, very big deal.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.150
We'll have more on this on our Instagram.

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:59.090
You can find The David Pakman Show on Instagram
by searching The David Pakman Show on Instagram.

